No, the difference is not merely in the name, but the "official" stamp
is important in directing user interest and network traffic to a
specific website and group of developers.  "The devteam" consequently
benefits from user buzz, prime access to bug reports (the pointer is
built into the distribution in many places, including the
executables), and their website acts as a central focus for the player
community.

You know as well as anyone that name and publicity matter in this
world.  Alternate developers who would wish to maintain the game on a
timely schedule, release their updates and publicize them are
disadvantaged because of the "official" imprimatur.  Patchhack, for
example, I am sure, does not enjoy anywhere near the user awareness
that www.nethack.org does, nor do users play it nearly as much, and
not because it is an inferior game.

The argument that "the devteam" can do anything they want and that we
can do anything we want because we have the source code and therefore
we should just shut up, is asinine.  Open source isn't a ticket to
sleep at the wheel.  The player community repeatedly shows strong
interest in updates and improvements to the game.  "The devteam" holds
the reins, and therefore has a responsibility to the player community,
whether they like it or not.  "The devteam" is moribund in their rate
of coding and release and in their communication.  The result, for a
long time now, has been a motley collection of web sites with this and
that disconnected and often incompatible patch or compatibility of
unknown status, duplication of effort by multiple people, overall
little central organization of ideas and labor, and therefore
decreased accessibility to players of game improvements.  All of these
problems would be addressed by administration of the project from a
user-transparent collaborative programming site, or at least
administration by a group of active developers.

Bottom line, if "the devteam" isn't going to do the work, turn it over
to a group of developers who will, and quit hiding lack of time or
desire, or laziness, behind the open source battle cry.


