I'm not sure that Hack and Rogue continued to be developed in time
periods overlapping with their successors.  Even if they were, note
that the games had to be rebranded and overhauled to attract a loyal
user base of their own.  There's also issues relating to a program's
life cycle and the fact that early development history is more
volatile and adaptable.  The current situation wrt nethack is not
comparable to that.  Players like nethack, it's well-established, and
it could keep thriving on its own with a development process long into
the future, if the caretakers of the game did the work.  We don't need
a new game here.

